<section id="s0">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">00</span><h2 class="sec">Purpose &amp; Scope</h2></div>
  <div class="sec-rule"></div>
  <p class="lede">The Marine Unit is the Park Rangers' tactical subdivision built for rough terrain &mdash; mountains, tunnels and shoreline the standard Ranger Tac element isn't built to work.</p>
  <p>It fields riflemen who hold high ground and stay unseen doing it, a breaching element trained on tunnels and culverts instead of buildings and doorways, and a Bearcat crew that pulls people out of ground nothing else on the roster can reach.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <span class="tag">Platform</span>
    <p>This is ER:LC (Roblox) roleplay content. Every technique below runs on the game's actual mechanics &mdash; nothing here assumes equipment or ordnance ER:LC doesn't have, and where the game is missing something, this handbook says so instead of inventing it.</p>
  </div>
  <p>This handbook covers ridge and mountain positioning for designated marksmen and riflemen, reading and breaching tunnels including solo entries, Bearcat-supported evacuation of civilians from hard-to-reach terrain, and a deliberately light placeholder for the waterborne element until ER:LC's boat system is updated.</p>
</section>

<section id="s1">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2 class="sec">Team Composition &amp; Roles</h2></div>
  <div class="sec-rule"></div>
  <p>Five to seven, depending on terrain. Never fewer than four on a tunnel job &mdash; below that you lose either the entry element or rear security.</p>
  <div class="tw">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Callsign</th><th>Role</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-1</td><td class="k">Team Leader</td><td>Calls the plan, the breach and the route. Runs the radio. Does not breach or take point.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-2</td><td class="k">Breacher</td><td>Reads the tunnel mouth or door, opens it, steps clear, becomes last man in.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-3</td><td class="k">Point</td><td>First through. Takes the angle you can't see from the mouth and holds it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-4</td><td class="k">Cover</td><td>Second through. Takes the opposite angle, watches Point's back.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-5</td><td class="k">Designated Marksman</td><td>Works the ridge or high ground above the objective. Does not enter tunnels. Calls movement the entry element can't see.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-6</td><td class="k">Bearcat Operator</td><td>Stages and drives the Bearcat. Stays with the vehicle unless called forward for an extraction.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">MU-7</td><td class="k">Coxswain</td><td>Boat element lead. Scope is placeholder for now &mdash; see &sect;07.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ul class="bul">
    <li>The DM slot is the highest-value position on a ridge job and the easiest to waste. One rifleman calling &ldquo;two subjects moved to the east face&rdquo; is worth more than putting a second team on the slope.</li>
    <li>The Bearcat operator's weapon stays on the vehicle, not in their hands &mdash; if MU-6 is off the truck, nobody is driving it out.</li>
    <li>Whoever reaches a downed person first becomes medical, and says so out loud.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="s2">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2 class="sec">Ridge &amp; Mountain Work</h2></div>
  <div class="sec-rule"></div>
  <p>Rangers already have the advantage here &mdash; green and tan that disappear into the terrain the Marine Unit works every day. Most teams waste it by walking the actual ridgeline, which is the one line on a mountain where you're visible from every direction at once.</p>

  <h3>Work the military crest, not the top</h3>
  <p>The skyline is the ridge's exact top edge &mdash; anyone below you on either side sees your whole outline against open sky the second you cross it. The military crest is the line just below that top edge, on the side facing your objective. From there you can still see down the slope, but you've got the mountain itself as a backdrop instead of sky.</p>

  <figure>
    <div class="plans">
      <div class="plan">
        <p class="cap">Skyline vs. military crest</p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 260 180" role="img" aria-label="Side view of a ridge: a rifleman on the crest is outlined against open sky, while a rifleman just below the crest has the mountain behind him.">
